  "reason": "Evidence: RNA-only with PCA (config_001) achieves 48.5% accuracy while image-only with ResNet50 (config_010) achieves only 20.8%, strongly indicating RNA is the dominant informative modality. This makes the choice of RNA representation the highest-leverage design decision. Uncertainty: We have only tested a linear PCA encoder (n2000). It is unknown whether a learned, nonlinear encoder (scvi_n200) captures biologically meaningful structure that PCA misses, which could substantially shift the performance ceiling. Question tested: config_004 is a clean single-variable comparison against config_001 (same rna_only model, same singleprofile setting; only the encoder changes from pca_n2000 to scvi_n200), directly isolating the effect of representation quality. Why best next step: Under a tight budget, establishing whether a purpose-built single-cell encoder outperforms PCA determines whether to invest further in encoder choice or instead focus on profile/aggregation/fusion dimensions. If scvi improves accuracy meaningfully, it reshapes the multimodal strategy; if not, PCA suffices and we can redirect budget toward multiprofile or fusion experiments."
